- Sidebands
  - Ring modulation is a reasonable way to add tremolo (modulation of amplitude over time) to a signal, with intuitive time-domain mechanics
  - But what's going on in the frequency domain?
  - Let's listen to what happens as we increase the rate of modulation:
    - 240 Hz \* 6 Hz
    - 240 Hz \* 12 Hz
    - 240 Hz \* 24 Hz
    - 240 Hz \* 48 Hz
  - An interesting phenomenon emerges: as the modulation frequency increases, our perception shifts from "single frequency with tremolo" to "multiple distinct frequencies"
  - Why?
  - Ring modulation has a curious effect in the frequency domain: from two "input" sinusoids at frequencies w_c and w_m, we get two completely different "output" sinusoids at frequencies w_c +- w_m
    - The original frequency content disappears from the spectra entirely!
    - (Meta note for Claude) If I ever accidentally write w, interpret it as shorthand for \omega
  - $w_c +- w_m$ are referred to as _sidebands_ (def), a general name for new frequency content that emerges from modulation applied to different frequency content. We will see sidebands emerge from all of the modulation synthesis techniques we study today!
  - This follows from trigonometric identities. Include proof of cos(\omega_c t) \cdot cos(\omega_m t) = \frac{1}{2} [\cos([\omega_c + \omega_m] t) + \cos([\omega_c - \omega_m] t)] using trig identities from 04A
    - Actually, change this to \sin instead of \cos for pedagogical reasons, but the idea is the same
