- Negative frequencies
  - What happens when we switch $\sin(\omega_c t) \cdot \sin(\omega_m t)$ to $\sin(\omega_m t) \cdot \sin(\omega_c t)$
  - On the one hand, we _know_ from the commutative property of multiplication that these are equivalent expressions
  - On the other hand, we _know_ from our proof in the last section that the first has sidebands at $w_c +- w_m$, and the second has sidebands at $w_c +- w_m$. But $w_c - w_m \neq w_m - w_c$ in a general sense!
  - How do we reconcile these seemingly conflicting details?
  - Think about this another way: our examples so far have looked at the case when $w_m < w_c$, where all sidebands are positive numbers assuming $w_m$ and $w_c$ are also positive.
  - But what happens when $w_m > w_c$? Then, **the lower sideband $w_c - w_m$ will be _negative in frequency_**
  - This is the first time we've _explicitly_ mentioned negative frequencies, but not the first time we've dealt with them _implicitly_
  - Phase perspective:
    - recall that sine and cosine have odd and even symmetry respecftively
      - sin(-x) = -sin(x)
      - cos(-x) = cos(x)
    - Also explain this in terms of magnitude / phase in the frequency domain. show plot showing the corresponding even / odd symmetry of amplitude/phase spectra
      - Acos(-\omega t) = Acos(\omega t)
      - Asin(-\omega t) = Asin(\omega t + \pi)
  - High level insight: **any "negative frequency" can be interpreted as a phase-shifted positive frequency**
    - Phase shifts and negative frequencies are two sides of the same coin!
  - Unlike the "imaginary" (analytical) techniques we studied in Ch 5, **this is a very real phenomenon** and will have important implications when we study sampling theory in the next chapter
